
                            A m i N e t X D u o

              A TCP/IP stack for AmigaOS, on Eclipse ThreadX NetX Duo


WHAT IT IS
----------

AmiNetXDuo gives AmigaOS the bsdsocket.library that every Amiga networking
program already speaks, over the SANA-II driver that every Amiga network card
already has.  It is a complete stack, not a shim: DHCP, ARP, ICMP, TCP, UDP,
DNS, and -- in the IPv6 build -- neighbour discovery, duplicate address
detection and stateless autoconfiguration.

It is MIT licensed throughout, with no 4.4BSD or GPL lineage anywhere in it.


BEFORE YOU START
----------------

You need:

  *  AmigaOS 2.04 or newer.
  *  Any 68000 or better.  Encrypted (https:) connections need a 68020; on a
     68000 they are left out and everything else works as normal.
  *  About 1 MB of free memory once it is up, and 4 MB in the machine.
  *  A network card, and that card's SANA-II driver -- a file whose name ends
     in ".device", which came with the card.  Put it in DEVS: before you
     install, and the installer will find it and pre-select your card for
     you.

Cards known to work this way, and the driver each one uses:

     Commodore A2065 . . . . . . . . . a2065.device
     Village Tronic Ariadne  . . . . . ariadne.device
     Village Tronic Ariadne II . . . . ariadne2.device
     Hydra Systems AmigaNet  . . . . . amiganet.device
     Individual Computers X-Surf . . . xsurf.device
     Individual Computers X-Surf 100 . xsurf100.device
     PCMCIA Ethernet (A600/A1200)  . . cnet.device
     3Com EtherLink III / 3C589  . . . etherlink3.device
     Prism2 wireless PCMCIA  . . . . . prism2.device

Any other SANA-II driver works too; the installer's last menu entry lets you
type its name.


INSTALLING
----------

Double-click "Install-AmiNetXDuo".

Its icon's default tool is "Installer", so Commodore's Installer must be
somewhere in your command path -- on a standard Workbench 3.1 it is, on the
Install disk and usually copied to C: or SYS:Utilities.  If Workbench says it
cannot find "Installer", set the icon's Default Tool (Workbench menu:
Icons/Information) to wherever your copy actually is.

From a Shell, the equivalent is:

     Stack 10000
     CD <the drawer this file is in>
     Installer SCRIPT Install-AmiNetXDuo APPNAME AmiNetXDuo

(Keyword and value separated by a space, not by "=".  Installer 2.17, the
one on the Workbench 3.1 Install disk, parses its command line by hand and
does not recognise "SCRIPT=name".  The CD matters too: started from a Shell
the script finds its files relative to the current directory.)

The installer asks which network card you have and whether your network hands
out addresses automatically.  If you do not know the answer to the second
one, say yes: nearly every home network does, and it is one line in one file
to change afterwards.

It puts

     bsdsocket.library, usergroup.library      into LIBS:
     NetSetup, AddNetInterface, Online, Offline,
     ShowNetStatus, ping, netstat, host,
     nslookup, arp, fetch, nc, telnet,
     NetTrace, sntp, traceroute, tftp, whois,
     CheckNetConfig, GetNetStatus, NetShutdown,
     AddNetRoute, DeleteNetRoute, ssh          into C:
     the interface description                 into DEVS:NetInterfaces
     the routing and name server settings      into DEVS:Internet
     this file, the documentation, examples    into a drawer you choose

and, if you let it, one line into S:User-Startup so the network comes up when
the machine boots.

Running the installer again over a working installation is safe.  It notices
the existing configuration and offers to leave it exactly as it is, and it
only ever replaces its own block in S:User-Startup.

If there is already a bsdsocket.library on the machine -- from AmiTCP, Miami,
Genesis or Roadshow -- the installer renames it to bsdsocket.library.old
rather than deleting it, and tells you it has done so.  Two TCP/IP stacks
cannot share one machine.


DID IT WORK?
------------

     ShowNetStatus              is it up, and what address did it get
     ping 192.168.1.1           can it reach your router
     host aminet.net            can it look a name up
     netstat -r                 what does it think the routes are

If ShowNetStatus says nothing is running, the usual reason is that the card's
driver is not in DEVS:.  The second most usual reason is the wrong card
chosen in the installer; run it again and pick another.


DOING IT BY HAND
----------------

Everything the installer writes is a plain text file you can write yourself.
There is a commented example of each of them in the Examples drawer:

     Examples/NetInterfaces/eth0        every keyword there is, explained
     Examples/NetInterfaces/a2065       the short version, one per card
     Examples/NetInterfaces/eth0-static a fixed address, worked through
     Examples/Internet/routes           the default route
     Examples/Internet/name_resolution  name servers and the host name
     Examples/Internet/hosts            names known without a name server

The minimum that works is three lines in DEVS:NetInterfaces/eth0:

     DEVICE=a2065.device
     UNIT=0
     CONFIGURE=DHCP

Note CONFIGURE, not IPTYPE.  Documentation elsewhere on the net that tells
you to write IPTYPE=DHCP is repeating an AmiTCP-era mistake: in Roadshow
IPTYPE is a number, the SANA-II packet type.  AmiNetXDuo accepts both, so
such a file still works here, but it will not work on Roadshow.


USING SSH
---------

`ssh' is Dropbear's dbclient.  It wants mathieeedoubbas.library in LIBS:,
which every Workbench install has, and nothing else -- it brings its own
stack, so there is no `stack 200000' to type first.

     ssh me@192.168.1.10                    log in
     ssh me@192.168.1.10 "uname -a"         run one command

A password works if the far end allows one.  A key is better, and on a
machine this slow it also saves the typing.

THE KEY MUST BE IN DROPBEAR'S FORMAT, not OpenSSH's.  An OpenSSH key copied
straight over will not be read.  On the PC, with Dropbear's own tools:

     dropbearkey -t ed25519 -f id_amiga     make one
     dropbearconvert openssh dropbear ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 id_amiga
                                            or convert one you already have
     dropbearkey -y -f id_amiga             print the line for the server's
                                            authorized_keys

MAKE THE KEY ON THE PC, not on the Amiga.  This machine has very little
entropy to make one from, and ed25519 keygen on a 14 MHz 68020 takes about a
minute.  Copy the private key over afterwards.

NAME THE KEY WITH -i, every time:

     ssh -i DH0:keys/id_amiga me@192.168.1.10

There is a default path -- dbclient reads ENV:HOME, appends /.ssh/id_dropbear,
and falls back to SYS: when ENV:HOME is unset -- but putting a key there and
leaving -i off was tried and does NOT work; the connection is refused with
"No auth methods could be used".  Use -i and it works every time.

ENV:USER is the name sent when the command line has no user@ in front of the
host; with none set it is "amiga".

     SetEnv SAVE USER me


UNINSTALLING
------------

     Delete LIBS:bsdsocket.library LIBS:usergroup.library
     Delete C:NetSetup C:AddNetInterface C:Online C:Offline
     Delete C:ShowNetStatus
     Delete C:ping C:netstat C:host C:nslookup C:arp C:fetch
     Delete C:nc C:telnet C:NetTrace
     Delete C:sntp C:traceroute C:tftp C:whois
     Delete C:CheckNetConfig C:GetNetStatus C:NetShutdown
     Delete C:AddNetRoute C:DeleteNetRoute C:ssh
     Delete DEVS:NetInterfaces/eth0

and take the ";BEGIN AmiNetXDuo" .. ";END AmiNetXDuo" block out of
S:User-Startup.  If you had another stack before, rename
LIBS:bsdsocket.library.old back over LIBS:bsdsocket.library.


STATUS
------

It gets a DHCP lease, answers ARP, pings its gateway, resolves DNS, moves TCP
in both directions and listens for incoming connections.  bsdsocket.library
scores 141 of 142 on the bsdsocktest conformance suite.

Everything above was measured on an emulated 68000, 68020 and 68030 under
Kickstart 2.04 and 3.1.  It has not run on real hardware.


LICENCE
-------

MIT.  ThreadX and NetX Duo are MIT as well (Microsoft / Eclipse ThreadX
contributors) and are used unmodified.

One file is not MIT.  DEVS:Internet/certificates -- present only in an archive
that also carries tls.library -- is the Mozilla root certificate set, which is
covered by the Mozilla Public License 2.0.  It is built, unmodified in
substance, from the dated snapshot at third_party/cacert/cacert.pem in the
source tree, itself a verbatim copy of https://curl.se/ca/cacert.pem; the
converter is tools/mkcertstore.py.  MPL 2.0 is file-scoped, so nothing else
here is affected, and third_party/cacert/README.md sets the reasoning out in
full.

No AmiTCP, AROSTCP, Miami or Roadshow code is used, copied or disassembled.
Olaf Barthel's freely distributable Roadshow SDK headers and autodocs were
used as an ABI reference only.
